      "title": "GPT-5.6 Luna vs. GPT-6 Astra: Is a $1.20 Model Good Enough for Code Review?",
      "url": "https://entelligence.ai/blogs/gpt-5.6-luna-vs-gpt-6-astra-is-a-1.20-model-good-enough-for-code-review",
      "discuss_url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49703003",
      "score": 147,
      "comments": 136,
      "author": "theanonymousone",
      "published": "2026-09-14T19:56:20Z",
      "bullets": [
        "Compares budget GPT-5.6 Luna against flagship GPT-6 Astra, weighing $1.20 cost-efficiency against reasoning depth in automated code review pipelines.",
        "Mid-tier models like Luna adequately handle routine linting, style, and syntax checks at significantly lower inference costs in CI/CD.",
        "Frontier Astra-class architectures remain critical for complex cross-file context, deep logic bugs, and nuanced security vulnerability detection."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "lobsters",
      "subsource": "lobste.rs/programming",
      "category": "engineering",
      "title": "Coreutils - rejected feature requests",
      "url": "https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/rejected_requests.html",
      "discuss_url": "https://lobste.rs/s/zbcua4",
      "score": 14,
      "comments": 2,
      "author": "ryan-duve",
      "published": "2026-09-15T09:06:36Z",
      "bullets": [
        "GNU Coreutils maintains an explicit rejected-features list to preserve POSIX conformance, minimalism, and long-term backwards compatibility.",
        "Frequently declined requests include built-in progress meters (e.g., for cp) and checksum variations, delegating tasks to tools like pv.",
        "Upstream maintainers prioritize Unix composability, rejecting redundant flags and interactive features that can be resolved via shell pipelines."
      ]
